#!/usr/bin/env bash
# grc-provision: how to prepare an Ubuntu TARGET host for auditing.
#
# The auditor never installs anything on a target (that's the audit-defensibility
# guarantee: a missing scanner is reported, never silently fixed). Target prep is
# a separate, explicit step the operator runs ON the target with the self-contained
# grc-target-setup script. This command just shows you how, and can print the
# script so you can pipe it over SSH.
here="$(cd "$(dirname "$(readlink -f "$0" 2>/dev/null || echo "$0")")" && pwd)"
. "$here/_grc-common.sh"
grc_load_env

script="$GRC_HOME/grc-target-setup"

[ -f "$script" ] || grc_die "target-setup script not found at $script -- re-run ./install.sh."

if [ "${1:-}" = "--print" ]; then
    cat "$script"
    exit 0
fi

cat <<EOF
grc-provision: prepare an Ubuntu TARGET to be audited.

The run host never modifies a target. Run the self-contained target script ON
the Ubuntu target instead. Two ways:

  # A) copy it over, then run it on the target (needs sudo there)
  scp "$script" <user>@<target>:/tmp/grc-target-setup
  ssh <user>@<target> 'sudo bash /tmp/grc-target-setup --scan-user grc-scan'

  # B) pipe it over SSH without copying a file
  grc-provision --print | ssh <user>@<target> 'sudo bash -s -- --scan-user grc-scan'

What it does on the target: installs oscap + the matching SCAP Security Guide
datastream for that Ubuntu release, and (with --scan-user NAME) creates a
passwordless-sudo scan account. Add --pubkey 'ssh-ed25519 AAAA... you' to
authorize your run-host key in one shot. Idempotent; verifies at the end.

Full walkthrough (host-key pinning, pre-flight AUTH_OK check): docs/validation.md.
Print the script:  grc-provision --print
EOF
